%% Revision 15 DOCUMENTED one decision, U1 the UUID generation strategy, and presented it as the owner's approval -
%%   it was not one: no owner approval had been given at that time. The owner approved U1 in revision 16 (overview 11
%%   item 34; the correction record is overview 12.15), so the six parts below stand as the approved strategy while the
%%   approval is attributed to revision 16. Like revisions 11-14 it
%%   adds no entity, no attribute line, no relationship and no key. What is approved (recorded in overview 11 item 33,
%%   approved in item 34), in six
%%   parts, is recorded here because every PK in this diagram is an identifier and the reader should know where the
%%   values come from:
%%     1. UUIDv7 for EVERY entity id, including the ones that leave the system: events.id is the event_id in the 202
%%        body, and deliveries.id is the X-HookFlow-Delivery-Id consumers dedupe on. Both stay uuid.
%%     2. Generated by the GO APPLICATION LAYER before the INSERT. No uuid column here has, or may have, a database
%%        DEFAULT - no gen_random_uuid(), no native uuidv7(), no trigger and no sequence behind an id. That is why
%%        this revision changes no DDL: the defaults were never in the design in the first place.
%%     3. ONE strategy shared by api, worker and outbox-publisher, so delivery_attempts.id and the NEW
%%        outbox_messages row that each retry step inserts are built exactly the way events.id is. Each row keeps the
%%        id it was created with: re-publishing or re-driving claims the existing row and UPDATEs its status, attempts
%%        and lease columns, and nothing in this design rewrites a row's own id (database-schema.md 4.10 and
%%        consequence 25). idempotency_records.event_id is a link to another row's key, not a key of its own, and the
%%        UPDATE that fills it in 5.1 step d is what U44's deferred trigger checks - a different thing entirely.
%%     4. The APPROXIMATE CREATION TIME a v7 value carries is ACCEPTED. An identifier is not an authorization
%%        credential: tenant isolation is the organization_id invariant and the "org+id target" unique keys below
%%        (section 3.1 of database-schema.md), plus API scopes - never the id's order, age or guessability.
%%     5. Business ordering and scheduling stay on the TIMESTAMP columns - received_at, created_at, next_attempt_at,
%%        available_at, expires_at. No relationship, index or scan uses an id as a business ordering key. A
%%        deterministic tie-breaker such as outbox_messages' (available_at, id) uses the id only after the timestamp
%%        and is not business ordering.
%%        Nothing here claims monotonicity either: RFC 9562 section 5.7 fills the 74 bits after the timestamp with
%%        random data by default and makes the counter and sub-millisecond sub-fields an optional MAY, so two ids from
%%        one generator inside one millisecond have no guaranteed relative order. No attribute, index or test in this
%%        design may depend on that order, and this round removed the monotonicity expectation that an earlier draft of
%%        overview 10 had written: with no library selected (part 6), monotonicity is a property to be read off a real
%%        implementation later, never a rule this diagram can assert.
%%     6. NO LIBRARY AND NO VERSION is chosen by this decision. Naming a uuid package or a dependency is a separate
%%        act that belongs with the open tooling and dependency questions; no go.mod exists, so nothing is named.
